<div class="page-break"></div>
<div style="text-align: right; font-size: 11pt; margin-bottom: 20pt;">1</div>
<h1>CHAPTER 1<br>INTRODUCTION</h1>
<br>

<h2>1.1 Background</h2>
<p>
Modern enterprise networks—particularly those operating in high-consequence environments such as smart university campuses, research centers, and hospital complexes—have evolved into extraordinarily dense, heterogeneous infrastructures. In medical centers, the campus local area network (LAN) and metropolitan area network (MAN) transport massive streams of mission-critical data. These include continuous patient telemetry from Intensive Care Units (ICU), real-time Picture Archiving and Communication System (PACS) DICOM imaging files from radiology suites, and emergency surgical telemetry. Any interruption, buffer buildup, or routing loop across these communication channels can compromise human life, disrupt clinical workflows, or violate strict service-level agreements (SLAs).
</p>
<p>
Traditionally, network operations centers (NOC) manage these infrastructures through legacy network monitoring systems (NMS) relying on the Simple Network Management Protocol (SNMP) and syslog daemons. SNMP periodically queries managed network elements (routers, switches, firewalls) at intervals typically spanning 30 to 60 seconds. While effective for historical capacity planning and post-mortem auditing, this periodic polling model is intrinsically <em>reactive</em>. Alarms are raised only after link utilization has peaked at 100%, interface buffers have overflowed, and thousands of frames have been dropped.
</p>

<h2>1.2 Motivations</h2>
<p>
The motivation for this project stems from three fundamental deficiencies in current enterprise network management:
</p>
<p>
<strong>1. Absence of Spatio-Temporal Awareness:</strong> Network failures do not occur in isolation. A failing physical transceiver or an overloaded switch creates buffer pressure that rapidly cascades along physical cabling upstream to distribution and core routers. Traditional threshold-based monitoring treats switches as independent silos, ignoring the physical graph topology.
</p>
<p>
<strong>2. Severe Telemetry Class Imbalance:</strong> Under normal operational circumstances, over 98% of telemetry samples reflect nominal, healthy states. When conventional machine learning models (such as Multi-Layer Perceptrons or Decision Trees) are trained on raw telemetry, they suffer from catastrophic majority-class bias, failing completely to identify rare, catastrophic failure events like broadcast storms.
</p>
<p>
<strong>3. High Latency of Manual Human Remediation:</strong> Once an alarm is detected by a traditional NMS, a human network engineer must manually log into the Command Line Interface (CLI) via SSH, interpret routing tables, and deploy reconfigurations. This manual Mean Time to Repair (MTTR) regularly exceeds 10 to 30 minutes—an unacceptable window for emergency medical networks.
</p>

<h2>1.3 Scope of the Project</h2>
<p>
This project designs, implements, and benchmarks a closed-loop <strong>Network Digital Twin (NDT)</strong> coupled with an <strong>Adaptive Software-Defined Networking (SDN)</strong> controller for proactive failure prediction and sub-second autonomous self-healing. The specific scope encompasses:
</p>
<ul>
    <li>Modeling a 30-node, 58-link 3-tier hierarchical hospital campus topology spanning 7 logical medical wards.</li>
    <li>Developing a hybrid Spatio-Temporal deep learning model: Feature-Temporal Squeeze LSTM (FTS-LSTM) for multi-variate telemetry sequence encoding and Message Passing Neural Networks (MPNN) for topological neighborhood message propagation.</li>
    <li>Applying Focal Loss ($\gamma = 2.0$) to guarantee robust detection of rare failure categories.</li>
    <li>Implementing an automated SDN remediation client that injects OpenFlow 1.3 flow modification commands.</li>
    <li>Delivering an enterprise-grade web NOC dashboard equipped with live topology visualization, interactive node inspection drawers, and on-demand fault injection testing controls.</li>
</ul>

