<h1>CHAPTER 2<br>PROJECT DESCRIPTION AND GOALS</h1>
<br>

<h2>2.1 Literature Review</h2>
<p>
Proactive network fault diagnosis and autonomous management have garnered intense interest across academic and industrial networking domains. Early literature centered primarily on statistical forecasting models such as Autoregressive Integrated Moving Average (ARIMA) and Holt-Winters exponential smoothing to anticipate traffic volume bursts. While statistically robust for univariate seasonal data, these models fail to capture non-linear cross-metric correlations between interface queue depth, CPU utilization, and packet drops.
</p>
<p>
With the emergence of deep learning, recurrent neural architectures, particularly Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) and Gated Recurrent Units (GRU), demonstrated high fidelity in modeling multi-variate time-series telemetry. However, purely temporal architectures are blind to network topology; an LSTM analyzing switch $S_5$ has no mathematical mechanism to incorporate the queue state of neighboring switch $S_2$.
</p>
<p>
More recently, Graph Neural Networks (GNNs), such as Graph Convolutional Networks (GCN) and Message Passing Neural Networks (MPNN), have emerged as the foundational paradigm for networking. By structuring the campus topology as an adjacency graph $G = (V, E)$, GNNs allow node features to propagate iteratively along physical communication edges, enabling the model to learn spatial failure cascade dynamics.
</p>

<h2>2.2 Research Gap</h2>
<p>
Despite significant individual advances in GNNs and SDN, existing implementations suffer from critical disconnects:
</p>
<ul>
    <li>Most predictive AI models are evaluated offline on static datasets without real-time closed-loop integration with an active SDN controller.</li>
    <li>Standard deep learning loss functions (e.g., categorical cross-entropy) fail under real-world class imbalance where failure events represent less than 2% of the dataset.</li>
    <li>Existing digital twin prototypes lack intuitive, production-grade visual interfaces that bridge deep learning predictions with operational NOC workflows.</li>
</ul>

