<!-- ==================== CHAPTER 4 ==================== -->
<div class="page-break"></div>
<div style="text-align: right; font-size: 11pt; margin-bottom: 20pt;">8</div>
<h1>CHAPTER 4<br>DESIGN APPROACH AND DETAILS</h1>
<br>

<h2>4.1 Proposed 4-Layer System Architecture</h2>
<p>
The proposed framework implements a continuous, closed-loop cyber-physical system divided into four interacting functional layers:
</p>

<h3>Layer 1: Telemetry Ingestion Layer</h3>
<p>
Interfaces directly with physical or emulated network elements. It continuously collects five primary telemetry features from each switch interface across a sliding window of $T = 15$ seconds at 1-second intervals:
</p>
<ul>
    <li>Bandwidth Utilization ($U \in [0, 100]\%$)</li>
    <li>Buffer Queue Depth ($Q \in [0, 100]\%$)</li>
    <li>Packet Drop Rate ($D$ in packets per second)</li>
    <li>Cyclic Redundancy Check (CRC) Error Count ($E$)</li>
    <li>Switch CPU / Memory Load ($C \in [0, 100]\%$)</li>
</ul>

<h3>Layer 2: Digital Twin Graph Topology Layer</h3>
<p>
Maintains an accurate, real-time property graph abstraction $G = (V, E)$ of the physical campus infrastructure. The vertex set $V$ contains 30 nodes (2 Core Routers, 6 Distribution Switches, 22 Access Switches). The edge set $E$ contains 58 bidirectional physical cabling links. Nodes are mapped into 7 compound ward clusters:
</p>
<ul>
    <li>Main Datacenter (Nodes 0, 1)</li>
    <li>Emergency Department (Nodes 2, 8, 9, 10)</li>
    <li>Intensive Care Unit (Nodes 3, 11, 12, 13)</li>
    <li>Radiology &amp; Imaging (Nodes 4, 14, 15, 16)</li>
    <li>Outpatient Clinic (Nodes 5, 17, 18, 19)</li>
    <li>Surgical Wing (Nodes 6, 20, 21, 22)</li>
    <li>Administrative &amp; Billing (Nodes 7, 23, ..., 29)</li>
</ul>

<h3>Layer 3: AI Spatio-Temporal Prediction Core</h3>
<p>
Operates on the streaming graph data using a two-stage hybrid deep neural network:
</p>
<p>
1. <strong>FTS-LSTM (Feature-Temporal Squeeze LSTM):</strong> Computes a 64-dimensional temporal embedding $h_v^{(0)}$ for each node $v$ by processing the $15 \times 5$ multi-variate time-series through temporal attention gates.
</p>
<p>
2. <strong>MPNN (Message Passing Neural Network):</strong> Propagates the temporal embeddings across topological graph edges over $K=2$ spatial hops. If switch $v$ experiences queue buildup, its neighbors aggregate this context:
</p>
<p style="text-align: center; font-family: 'Courier New', monospace;">
m_v^(k) = \sum_{u \in N(v)} M_k(h_v^(k-1), h_u^(k-1), e_{u,v})
</p>
<p style="text-align: center; font-family: 'Courier New', monospace;">
h_v^(k) = U_k(h_v^(k-1), m_v^(k))
</p>
<p>
3. <strong>Focal Loss Multi-Class Classifier:</strong> Classifies each node into one of six failure states using dynamic class reweighting to conquer class imbalance.
</p>

<h3>Layer 4: Autonomous SDN Remediation &amp; NOC Web UI</h3>
<p>
When an impending failure is predicted, this layer maps the failure class into an automated OpenFlow flow modification directive:
</p>
<ul>
    <li><em>Uplink Congestion (Class 1):</em> Dispatches an <code>OFPFC_MODIFY</code> flow-mod rule with <code>SET_QUEUE</code> to reroute bulk traffic to a secondary path.</li>
    <li><em>Hardware Degradation (Class 2):</em> Dispatches an <code>OFPPC_PORT_DOWN</code> administrative command to failover active sessions to redundant links.</li>
    <li><em>Broadcast Storm (Class 3):</em> Injects a high-priority <code>OFPFC_ADD</code> packet drop rule matching rogue ARP broadcast ingress ports.</li>
</ul>

<!-- ==================== CHAPTER 5 ==================== -->
<div class="page-break"></div>
<div style="text-align: right; font-size: 11pt; margin-bottom: 20pt;">10</div>
<h1>CHAPTER 5<br>METHODOLOGY AND TESTING</h1>
<br>

<h2>5.1 Module Descriptions</h2>

<h3>1. Feature-Temporal Squeeze LSTM (`fts_lstm.py`)</h3>
<p>
Standard LSTMs incur heavy computational overhead when processing multi-variate metrics across dozens of distributed switches. FTS-LSTM introduces a squeeze-and-excitation temporal attention gate that compresses the feature dimension prior to recurrence, isolating salient degradation trends (such as climbing CRC error rates) while minimizing inference latency.
</p>

<h3>2. Message Passing Neural Network (`mpnn.py`)</h3>
<p>
The MPNN module utilizes PyTorch Geometric message-passing primitives. In each iteration, nodes construct messages comprising their current hidden state and edge weights (link capacity, current utilization). The aggregated messages update the node representations, allowing the model to distinguish between localized benign bursts and systemic cascading bottlenecks.
</p>

<h3>3. Focal Loss Function (`losses.py`)</h3>
<p>
To address the fact that healthy network operations represent over 98% of the training distribution, Focal Loss reshapes the standard cross-entropy loss by introducing a modulating factor $(1 - p_t)^\gamma$:
</p>
<p style="text-align: center; font-family: 'Courier New', monospace;">
FL(p_t) = -\alpha_t (1 - p_t)^\gamma \log(p_t)
</p>
<p>
Setting $\gamma = 2.0$ reduces the relative loss contribution of easily classified nominal samples ($p_t > 0.9$), forcing the gradient optimizer to focus exclusively on rare, catastrophic failure classes.
</p>

<h3>4. SDN Remediation Controller (`sdn_remediation_controller.py`)</h3>
<p>
This module acts as the autonomous translation bridge between deep learning predictions and OpenFlow 1.3 flow table modification rules. It maintains a dictionary of pre-computed SDN intents and exposes REST endpoints for automated or operator-authorized execution.
</p>

<h2>5.2 Test Plan and Validation Matrix</h2>
<p>
The system was validated using a rigorous multi-scenario test suite designed to verify detection accuracy, prediction lead time, and closed-loop recovery speed.
</p>

