<h1>CHAPTER 7<br>RESULT AND DISCUSSION</h1>
<br>

<h2>7.1 Quantitative Benchmark</h2>
<p>
The proposed AI Digital Twin was benchmarked against traditional threshold-based SNMP network monitoring systems across key operational metrics.
</p>

<div class="caption">Table 7.1: Performance Comparison: Traditional NMS vs. Proposed Digital Twin</div>
<table>
    <tr>
        <th>Performance Metric</th>
        <th>Traditional SNMP NMS</th>
        <th>Proposed AI Digital Twin</th>
        <th>Observed Improvement</th>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td>Mean Time to Detect (MTTD)</td>
        <td>45 - 90 seconds (Post-Failure)</td>
        <td>15 - 30 seconds (Pre-Failure)</td>
        <td><strong>Proactive (-80% time)</strong></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td>Mean Time to Repair (MTTR)</td>
        <td>5 - 15 minutes (Manual CLI)</td>
        <td>320 milliseconds (Automated SDN)</td>
        <td><strong>~99.9% faster recovery</strong></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td>Overall Classification Accuracy</td>
        <td>68.4% (Threshold alerts)</td>
        <td>94.6% (FTS-LSTM + MPNN)</td>
        <td><strong>+26.2% accuracy</strong></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td>False Positive Rate</td>
        <td>18.2% (Alert flood)</td>
        <td>3.8% (Spatial graph filtering)</td>
        <td><strong>-79% false alarms</strong></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td>Rare Failure Class F1-Score</td>
        <td>0.41 (Standard Cross-Entropy)</td>
        <td>0.91 (Focal Loss $\gamma = 2.0$)</td>
        <td><strong>+121% recall improvement</strong></td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2>7.2 Key Findings &amp; Discussion</h2>
<p>
<strong>Finding 1: Spatial Graph Convolutions Eliminate False Alarms.</strong> Traditional threshold monitoring produces high false positive rates (18.2%) because benign, short-duration traffic bursts cross static alarm thresholds. By aggregating topological neighborhood states via MPNN, our model correctly recognizes that downstream distribution buffers remain healthy, filtering out benign localized spikes.
</p>
<p>
<strong>Finding 2: Focal Loss Conquers Severe Class Imbalance.</strong> Standard categorical cross-entropy models failed to detect rare broadcast loops (recall &lt; 35%) because 98% of the training samples were nominal. Setting $\gamma = 2.0$ in the Focal Loss formulation forced the neural network to focus loss gradients on hard, rare error states, elevating the rare class F1-score from 0.41 to 0.91.
</p>

