<h1>CHAPTER 8<br>CONCLUSION AND FUTURE WORK</h1>
<br>

<h2>8.1 Summary of Contributions</h2>
<p>
This project successfully designed, implemented, and benchmarked an end-to-end closed-loop Network Digital Twin and Adaptive SDN Remediation framework for enterprise campus networks. Key contributions include:
</p>
<ul>
    <li>Engineered a hybrid FTS-LSTM + MPNN spatio-temporal deep neural architecture capable of predicting network failures 15 to 30 seconds before link crash.</li>
    <li>Formulated a Focal Loss training protocol ($\gamma = 2.0$) resolving severe 98:2 class imbalance in streaming network telemetry.</li>
    <li>Constructed an automated OpenFlow SDN controller executing sub-second remediation (320ms recovery time).</li>
    <li>Developed a full-stack Web NOC dashboard with Cytoscape.js compound layout and Chart.js telemetry drawers.</li>
</ul>

<h2>8.2 Limitations and Future Work</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Physical Hardware Testbeds:</strong> Future extensions will evaluate the controller across physical P4-programmable hardware switches using In-band Network Telemetry (INT).</li>
    <li><strong>Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning:</strong> Extending the remediation engine from pre-defined policies to dynamic, multi-path reinforcement learning (Q-routing) across multi-campus wide area networks.</li>
    <li><strong>Cybersecurity Anomaly Detection:</strong> Broadening the classification vocabulary to include Distributed Denial of Service (DDoS) floods, ARP cache poisoning, and rogue DHCP servers.</li>
</ul>

